   "feature_image": "assets/img/calendar.jpg",
   "feature_caption": "Fig. 01 — The lot in autumn: the calendar keeps turning, and the center's work turns with it.",
   "interactive": true,
   "body": "<h1>The Annual Operating Calendar: A Year in the Life of a Shopping Center</h1>\n<p>Every shopping center runs the same annual loop: budgets, reconciliations, renewals, inspections, storm seasons, holidays, and year-end close. Owners who know the loop experience it as a rhythm; owners who do not experience it as a series of ambushes — the reconciliation deadline that appears from nowhere, the insurance renewal negotiated in a hurry, the holiday parking plan improvised in November. This article maps the operating year for a multi-tenant retail center, month by month, and explains how to turn the map into a calendar your operation actually runs on. Use the interactive explorer below to walk the year.</p>\n<h2>Why the year has a shape</h2>\n<p>Three forces shape the calendar. <strong>Finance</strong> sets the winter rhythm: the prior year's CAM reconciliation, the new budget, insurance and tax milestones. <strong>Weather</strong> sets the seasonal rhythm: in Gulf South markets, storm preparation dominates late spring and early summer, while exterior work clusters in the mild months. <strong>Retail</strong> sets the fall rhythm: back-to-school leasing, holiday traffic planning, and January's post-holiday reset. Most missed deadlines in property management are not knowledge failures; they are calendar failures — the task was known, but nothing surfaced it at the right moment.</p>\n<h2>The calendar</h2>\n<div class=\"ix\" data-ix=\"calendar\"></div>\n<h2>Working the calendar</h2>\n<p>The calendar only works as a system if three things are true. First, every item above that involves a lease obligation — reconciliation deadlines, insurance requirements, notice windows — must be verified against your actual lease language, because lease terms control over any generic calendar. Second, each monthly block needs a named owner and a defined artifact: not &quot;check the roof&quot; but &quot;file the spring roof inspection memo in the safe.&quot; Third, the calendar should live where the reminders live. If your system fires alerts from the same records that hold the documents, the calendar maintains itself; if it lives on paper, someone must carry it deliberately.</p>\n<h3>Regional adjustment</h3>\n<p>This calendar reflects a Gulf South rhythm — storm season in June, mild winters, exterior work in shoulder seasons. Other regions transpose it: freeze-market centers front-load winterization and push exterior work into summer; desert markets plan around heat rather than storms. The structure — finance in winter, maintenance by season, retail by the holiday clock — survives the move even when the months do not.</p>\n<h2>The view from Arnold Blvd</h2>\n<p>On The Blvd Shopping Center is a roughly 70,000 SF legacy center in Lafayette, and its year is the Gulf South version of the calendar above: reconciliation and budget season through winter, exterior and site work in the mild months, storm preparation complete before June, and the holiday rhythm anchored by the center's tenants. A legacy asset adds one more annual item worth naming: the walk-through audit. Once a year, walk every suite line, every roof section, every parking aisle, and every sign face with the checklist from Article 36 — because on an older property, the year always has one surprise, and the calendar's job is to make sure it is a small one.</p>\n<h2>The practical next step</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy the twelve monthly blocks above into your own system, with owners and dates.</li>\n<li>Verify every lease-driven date against actual lease language this month — especially reconciliation and insurance deadlines.</li>\n<li>Schedule the recurring inspections (roof, HVAC, fire-safety, signage) for the full year now, while contractors still have open calendars.</li>\n<li>Set the storm-prep or winterization block 90 days ahead of your region's season, not 30.</li>\n<li>Put the year-end close on the calendar today: December's work is won or lost in October's preparation.</li>\n</ol>\n"
